# 🚀 WordPress Backend Coding Standards

## ✅ Architecture

- [ ] Follow WordPress coding standards (hooks, filters)
- [ ] Avoid modifying core files
- [ ] Use plugins for custom functionality
- [ ] Keep logic modular and reusable

---

## ✅ Database & Queries

- [ ] Use $wpdb safely (prepare statements)
- [ ] Avoid direct SQL unless necessary
- [ ] Use WordPress functions (WP_Query, get_posts)
- [ ] Avoid N+1 queries
- [ ] Cache queries using transients

---

## ✅ Performance

- [ ] Minimize database calls
- [ ] Use object caching where possible
- [ ] Avoid heavy operations on page load
- [ ] Optimize queries and loops

---

## ✅ Security

- [ ] Sanitize all inputs (`sanitize_text_field`, etc.)
- [ ] Escape outputs (`esc_html`, `esc_attr`)
- [ ] Use nonces for forms
- [ ] Prevent SQL injection (use prepare)

---

## ✅ Code Quality

- [ ] Follow WordPress naming conventions
- [ ] Keep functions small and modular
- [ ] Add proper comments (PHPDoc)
- [ ] Avoid duplicate code

---

## ✅ Hooks & Filters

- [ ] Use hooks instead of modifying core logic
- [ ] Keep hooks lightweight
- [ ] Remove unused hooks

---

## ✅ Debugging & Logging

- [ ] Use WP_DEBUG for development only
- [ ] Log errors properly
- [ ] Remove debug logs in production

---

## 🚫 Avoid

- [ ] Direct SQL without prepare
- [ ] Hardcoding URLs/paths
- [ ] Editing core files
- [ ] Unescaped output